On the Gigabyte Z390 AORUS MASTER, fan headers are split across two separate Super I/O controller chips: the primary ITE IT8688E and an auxiliary ITE IT8792E. [1, 2, 3] 
Because third-party programs like [Fan Control](https://github.com/Rem0o/FanControl.Releases/issues/69) or AIDA64 often struggle to read the secondary chip, matching the physical motherboard headers to the correct controller chip is essential: [1, 2] 
## 🛠️ Controller to Header Mapping

* 
* ITE IT8688E (Primary Chip)
* CPU_FAN (Top right, next to RAM)
   * CPU_OPT (Top right, next to CPU_FAN)
   * SYS_FAN1 (Bottom edge, near front-panel headers)
   * SYS_FAN2 (Bottom edge, near front-panel headers)
   * SYS_FAN3 (Bottom edge, near USB headers) [1, 4, 5, 6, 7] 
* ITE IT8792E (Secondary Chip)
* SYS_FAN4 (Right edge, next to the 24-pin ATX power connector)
   * SYS_FAN5_PUMP (Bottom edge, left of SYS_FAN3)
   * SYS_FAN6_PUMP (Bottom edge, left of SYS_FAN5_PUMP) [1, 2, 4] 
* 

------------------------------
## ⚠️ Software Issues & Workarounds

* 
* The Problem: Third-party fan control programs usually only detect and control the fans hooked up to the IT8688E chip (CPU_FAN, CPU_OPT, SYS_FAN1, 2, 3). If you hook case fans up to SYS_FAN4, 5, or 6, the software will either report static readings or won't change their speeds at all. [1, 2] 
* The Solution: If you plan on using software to manage your curves, use PWM fan splitters to group your chassis fans together and plug them strictly into SYS_FAN1, SYS_FAN2, or SYS_FAN3. [8, 9] 
* The Exception: If you must use headers 4, 5, or 6, manage their fan profiles natively through Smart Fan 5 inside the motherboard BIOS, which controls both chips perfectly. [1, 10] 
* 

