When using [CoolerControl](https://docs.coolercontrol.org/) on Linux, it relies entirely on the kernel's hwmon driver subsystem to view and control fans. [1]
On your Gigabyte Z390 AORUS MASTER, the primary IT8688E will usually map to hwmon4, and the secondary IT8792E will map to hwmon5 (though the exact numbers can vary depending on your kernel and other connected devices like AIOs or GPUs). [2, 3]
Because Gigabyte hardware uses non-standard implementations for these dual ITE chips, you must configure a specific kernel module bypass and apply a unique BIOS trick to get CoolerControl to manage them. [4, 5]
## 1. Force Linux to Load Both Chips
By default, the Linux kernel refuses to touch the secondary chip because the motherboard's ACPI firmware claims the memory resource. You must force the it87 driver to ignore this resource conflict. [5, 6]

   1. Open your terminal and add the ACPI lax parameter to your boot loader. For GRUB, edit the configuration file:

   sudo nano /etc/default/grub

   2. Find the line starting with GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT and append acpi_enforce_resources=lax inside the quotes:

   GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="quiet splash acpi_enforce_resources=lax"

   3. Save the file and update your bootloader:

   sudo update-grub

   4. Next, configure the driver behavior. Create or edit the it87 modprobe file:

   sudo nano /etc/modprobe.d/it87.conf

   5. Add the following line to allow the driver to bind to both the IT8688E and IT8792E chips simultaneously:

   options it87 ignore_resource_conflict=1

   6. Reboot your computer. [6]

## 2. Apply the Gigabyte BIOS Trick (Crucial)
Gigabyte motherboards feature a hardware lock. If a fan header is set to "Auto," "Normal," or a fixed speed profile in Smart Fan 5, the motherboard firmware blocks CoolerControl from overriding the PWM signals. [4, 6, 7]

   1. Reboot your PC and enter the BIOS (press Delete). [8]
   2. Open Smart Fan 5.
   3. For every fan header you intend to control with CoolerControl, change its profile from "Normal" or "Silent" to Manual. [4]
   4. Set up a dummy 7-point fan curve. Ensure the final point on the curve is set to escalate to 100% duty cycle at higher temperatures (e.g., 70°C+). [7]
   5. Save settings and boot into Linux. This specific manual curve setup signals the ITE chips to yield manual PWM controls (pwm_enable=1) over to the operating system. [7, 9, 10, 11]

## 3. Map the Hardware in CoolerControl
Once you open the CoolerControl GUI, both chips will display under your local system devices. You can identify which target header maps to each chip path: [12]

| Motherboard Header | Linux Device Path Example | Controller Chip Source |
|---|---|---|
| CPU_FAN | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/pwm1 | IT8688E (Primary) |
| CPU_OPT | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/pwm5 | IT8688E (Primary) |
| SYS_FAN1 | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/pwm2 | IT8688E (Primary) |
| SYS_FAN2 | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/pwm3 | IT8688E (Primary) |
| SYS_FAN3 | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon4/pwm4 | IT8688E (Primary) |
| SYS_FAN4 | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon5/pwm1 | IT8792E (Secondary) |
| SYS_FAN5_PUMP | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon5/pwm2 | IT8792E (Secondary) |
| SYS_FAN6_PUMP | /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon5/pwm3 | IT8792E (Secondary) |

Note: If your liquid cooling pump is connected to SYS_FAN5 or SYS_FAN6, it is highly recommended to flag those specific headers as Unmanaged in CoolerControl to let the BIOS handle them safely at a constant high voltage. [7, 13]
