On the Gigabyte Z390 AORUS MASTER, the physical temperature sensors are split across the same two Super I/O controllers. [1, 2]
Because the it87 driver dynamically numbers things as temp1, temp2, etc., based on the chip it connects to, you have to look at the hwmon chip index to determine the actual motherboard location. [1, 3, 4]
The specific mapping for the default kernel driver configuration breaks down as follows:
## 🌡️ ITE IT8688E (Primary Chip / Typically hwmon4)
The primary controller handles the core motherboard components, including the internal VRM and PCIe tracking circuits. [1]

*
* temp1 (System 1): Main motherboard ambient sensor. Located near the bottom-left edge of the board, just above the audio capacitors.
* temp2 (Chipset / PCH): Located directly under the large metal AORUS heatsink covering the Intel Z390 Southbridge chipset.
* temp3 (CPU Socket): A sensor placed directly underneath the physical CPU bracket on the motherboard PCB (not the internal CPU core temp).
* temp4 (PCIe x16 Slot): Placed right beneath the primary graphics card slot to monitor ambient graphics card exhaust heat.
* temp5 (VRM MOS): Tracks the temperature of the main 12-phase CPU power delivery phases. It is embedded directly under the heavily-finned VRM heatsinks.
* temp6 (VSOC MOS): Tracks the temperature of the SoC/Uncore voltage phase rails, located under the top VRM heatsink block. [5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
*

------------------------------
## 🌡️ ITE IT8792E (Secondary Chip / Typically hwmon5)
The auxiliary chip handles general chassis ambient tracking and external expansion ports, including the physical headers you can plug liquid-cooling probes into. [10, 11]

*
* temp1 (System 2): General secondary motherboard ambient loop. Located near the right edge of the board, below the 24-pin ATX power connector.
* temp2 (EC_TEMP1 Header): The lower physical 2-pin header on the bottom edge of the board (near the front panel connector). It tracks whatever external thermal probe wire you plug into it. [10, 12]
* temp3 (EC_TEMP2 Header): The upper physical 2-pin header located near the right edge of the board (close to the SATA ports). It reads an external probe wire. [10]
*

------------------------------
## 💡 Linux Verification Trick
If you are ever unsure whether hwmon4 or hwmon5 shifted positions on a system reboot, run this command in your terminal:

sensors

Look at the headers labeled it8688-... and it8792-....

*
* The chip showing temp5 sitting around 40°C–60°C under load is your VRM (it8688).
* If temp2 or temp3 under the it8792 block reads exactly -128°C or 0°C, those are your EC_TEMP headers with nothing physically plugged into them. [10]
